-- Q1: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares with treasury stock repurchase mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Industries had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On September 1, 2025, the company repurchased 60,000 shares as treasury stock. Net income for the year was $700,000 with no preferred stock outstanding. What is basic EPS for 2025?', '["$2.50", "$2.33", "$2.92", "$2.41"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS uses weighted-average common shares outstanding. Shares are weighted: 300,000 for 8/12 plus 240,000 for 4/12 = 200,000 + 80,000 = 280,000. Basic EPS = $700,000 / 280,000 = $2.50. Choice B ($2.33) incorrectly uses all 300,000 shares as if no repurchase occurred, while choice C ($2.92) uses only the 240,000 ending balance without weighting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Effect of mid-period actual conversion of convertible preferred on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverton Corp. had 200,000 common shares outstanding all year and net income of $960,000. On January 1, 2025, 5,000 shares of $100 par, 8% cumulative convertible preferred stock were outstanding. On July 1, 2025, all preferred shares converted into 25,000 common shares. What is the effect of the mid-year conversion on Silverton''s basic EPS compared to no conversion occurring?', '["Basic EPS increases because the preferred dividend deduction is reduced by half", "Basic EPS decreases because the denominator increases more than the numerator", "Basic EPS is unaffected because the conversion is ignored in basic EPS", "Basic EPS increases because the full preferred dividend is eliminated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, actual conversions adjust both the numerator and denominator. Without conversion: ($960,000 - $40,000) / 200,000 = $4.60. With conversion: preferred deduction = $40,000 x 6/12 = $20,000; shares = 200,000 + (25,000 x 6/12) = 212,500; EPS = $940,000 / 212,500 = $4.42. The denominator increase outweighs the numerator gain, decreasing EPS. Choice A ignores the denominator effect of adding 12,500 weighted shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Warrants in treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Manufacturing has 150,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and net income of $525,000. The company has 12,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $15 per share. The average market price during the year was $45 per share. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.50", "$3.00", "$3.32", "$3.18"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, warrants use the treasury stock method identically to stock options. Proceeds = 12,000 x $15 = $180,000. Shares repurchased at market = $180,000 / $45 = 4,000. Incremental shares = 12,000 - 4,000 = 8,000. Diluted EPS = $525,000 / (150,000 + 8,000) = $525,000 / 158,000 = $3.32. Choice A ($3.50) incorrectly uses only the original 150,000 shares, ignoring the dilutive effect of the warrants entirely.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — Participating securities and two-class method overview
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. has 400,000 common shares and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that shares equally in dividends with common stock after receiving its 6% stated rate on $100 par. Net income is $1,800,000 and total declared dividends are $600,000. Under the two-class method required by ASC 260-10-45-60A, how does the participation feature affect the computation of basic EPS compared to a standard preferred deduction?', '["Undistributed earnings are allocated entirely to common shareholders", "Only the stated preferred dividend rate is deducted from the numerator", "The preferred deduction equals declared dividends only, with no allocation of undistributed earnings", "Undistributed earnings are allocated between common and preferred based on participation rights"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, participating securities require the two-class method. Declared dividends are allocated first; then undistributed earnings ($1,800,000 - $600,000 = $1,200,000) are allocated proportionally between common and preferred based on their participation rights. This reduces the numerator available to common shareholders beyond a simple preferred dividend deduction. Choice A is incorrect because undistributed earnings must be shared with participating preferred holders.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Retroactive adjustment for post-period stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Inc. reported basic EPS of $4.00 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares. On February 15, 2026 (before the financial statements are issued), Westbrook declared a 25% stock dividend. What basic EPS should Westbrook report in its 2025 financial statements?', '["$3.20", "$4.00", "$5.00", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in EPS. The weighted-average shares are adjusted to 200,000 x 1.25 = 250,000. Restated basic EPS = $800,000 / 250,000 = $3.20. Choice B ($4.00) incorrectly ignores the post-period stock dividend, but ASC 260 requires retroactive treatment to maintain period-to-period comparability.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Evaluate mandatorily convertible preferred for dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cascadia Energy has basic EPS of $5.00 (net income $1,500,000, 300,000 shares). The company issued 10,000 shares of mandatorily convertible preferred stock on January 1 that pays a $12 annual dividend per share and mandatorily converts into 40,000 common shares on December 31, 2027. The CFO argues that because conversion is mandatory, these shares should be excluded from diluted EPS until the conversion date. Evaluate the CFO''s position under ASC 260.', '["The CFO is correct; mandatorily convertible shares are excluded until conversion date", "The CFO is incorrect; mandatorily convertible shares must be included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method", "The CFO is correct; mandatory conversion eliminates the need for a diluted EPS computation", "The shares should be treated as contingently issuable and included only when conditions are met"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, mandatorily convertible securities must be included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method from issuance, regardless of when conversion occurs. The per-share impact is $120,000 / 40,000 = $3.00, which is less than basic EPS of $5.00, confirming the security is dilutive. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 treats mandatorily convertible instruments as potentially dilutive from the issuance date. Choice D confuses mandatorily convertible with contingently issuable shares.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Definition of simple capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best describes a simple capital structure?', '["A structure with both common stock and convertible securities outstanding", "A structure with common and preferred stock regardless of conversion features", "A structure with only common stock and no potentially dilutive securities outstanding", "A structure with fewer than 100,000 total shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has only common stock outstanding and no potentially dilutive securities such as convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock, options, or warrants. Entities with simple capital structures report only basic EPS. Choice A describes a complex capital structure because convertible securities are potentially dilutive and trigger the dual-presentation requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — EPS line items required on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which EPS figures must entities with complex capital structures present on the face of the income statement?', '["Basic EPS only, presented on the face of the statement", "Diluted EPS only, presented on the face of the statement", "Basic EPS on the face and diluted EPS disclosed in notes only", "Both basic and diluted EPS presented with equal prominence"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement. This dual presentation ensures investors can assess both current earnings attributable to common shares and the potential dilutive impact of outstanding securities. Choice C is incorrect because diluted EPS must appear on the face of the income statement, not in footnotes alone.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Effect of reverse stock split on reported EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornberry Ltd. had 500,000 shares outstanding and net income of $750,000 in 2025, yielding basic EPS of $1.50. On March 1, 2026, before the 2025 financial statements were issued, Thornberry executed a 1-for-5 reverse stock split. What effect does this reverse split have on Thornberry''s 2025 reported basic EPS?', '["Basic EPS is retroactively restated to $7.50", "Basic EPS remains $1.50 because the split occurred in a subsequent period", "Basic EPS is restated to $3.00 reflecting a partial-year adjustment", "Basic EPS is restated to $0.30 because shares increase fivefold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, reverse stock splits occurring after the reporting date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively applied. Adjusted shares = 500,000 / 5 = 100,000. Restated EPS = $750,000 / 100,000 = $7.50. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive treatment of all stock splits and reverse splits to ensure period-to-period comparability, regardless of timing relative to the balance sheet date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Convertible bonds actually converted mid-year: basic vs diluted treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. had 180,000 common shares and net income of $900,000 in 2025. On January 1, $500,000 of 6% convertible bonds were outstanding (each $1,000 bond converts to 8 shares). On July 1, 2025, all bondholders converted. The tax rate is 25%. How does the actual mid-year conversion affect basic EPS versus diluted EPS?', '["Both basic and diluted EPS ignore the conversion entirely", "Basic EPS includes converted shares for half the year; diluted EPS assumes conversion for the full year", "Basic EPS excludes the converted shares; diluted EPS includes them for the full year only", "Both basic and diluted EPS include converted shares for the half-year period only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, when bonds are actually converted, basic EPS includes the resulting common shares weighted from the conversion date (July 1 = 6/12). For diluted EPS, the if-converted method assumes conversion from January 1, adding shares for the full year and adding back after-tax interest for the pre-conversion period. Choice A is incorrect because actual share issuances must be reflected in basic EPS from the date they occur.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — Weighted-average shares with shares issued for services
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarfield Consulting had 100,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, 2025, the company issued 20,000 shares to an executive as compensation for services. Net income was $330,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.30", "$2.75", "$3.00", "$2.87"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for services are included in the weighted-average from the issuance date. Weighted-average shares = 100,000(6/12) + 120,000(6/12) = 50,000 + 60,000 = 110,000. Basic EPS = $330,000 / 110,000 = $3.00. Choice A ($3.30) incorrectly uses only the original 100,000 shares, while choice B ($2.75) incorrectly uses the ending balance of 120,000 for the entire year.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares when conditions NOT met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harmon Technologies has 250,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $1,125,000. Under an acquisition agreement, Harmon must issue 30,000 additional shares if cumulative revenues exceed $20 million by December 31, 2025. Actual cumulative revenues through 2025 are $17.5 million. How are the contingent shares treated in 2025 basic and diluted EPS?', '["Included in both basic and diluted EPS because the agreement exists", "Excluded from both basic and diluted EPS regardless of circumstances", "Included in basic EPS but excluded from diluted EPS", "Excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS if the effect is dilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all conditions are satisfied by the reporting date. Since revenue ($17.5M) has not reached the $20M threshold, shares are excluded from basic EPS. Under ASC 260-10-45-50, for diluted EPS, contingently issuable shares may still be included if conditions would be met assuming the period-end is the contingency end date. Choice B is incorrect because diluted EPS may still include contingent shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Evaluate multiple potentially dilutive securities ordering (most dilutive first)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Holdings has basic EPS of $4.00 based on $800,000 net income and 200,000 shares. Three convertible securities exist: (A) $200,000 of 4% bonds converting to 5,000 shares (tax rate 25%); (B) 1,000 preferred shares with $20 annual dividend converting to 8,000 common shares; (C) $400,000 of 12% bonds converting to 8,000 shares (tax rate 25%). Evaluate the per-share impact ranking and determine diluted EPS.', '["Include A and B only; diluted EPS is $3.88", "Include all three; diluted EPS is $3.79", "Include A only; diluted EPS is $3.93", "Exclude all three; diluted EPS equals basic EPS of $4.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, securities are ranked by per-share impact (lowest first). A: ($8,000 x 0.75)/5,000 = $1.20. B: $20,000/8,000 = $2.50. C: ($48,000 x 0.75)/8,000 = $4.50. Order: A, B, C. Adding A: $806,000/205,000 = $3.93 (dilutive). Adding B: $826,000/213,000 = $3.88 (dilutive). Testing C: per-share $4.50 exceeds cumulative $3.88, so C is antidilutive and excluded. Choice B is incorrect because including C would increase rather than decrease EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — What is the denominator in basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What measure of shares outstanding is used as the denominator in computing basic earnings per share under ASC 260?', '["Total shares authorized in the corporate charter", "Weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period", "Shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "Fully diluted shares including all potentially dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the denominator for basic EPS is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period. This time-weighted approach ensures shares are counted only for the portion of the period they were outstanding. Choice C is incorrect because ending shares would overstate or understate the denominator when shares were issued or repurchased mid-period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Stock buyback effect on basic EPS trend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Copperfield Corp. had net income of $2,000,000 in both 2024 and 2025 with 500,000 shares outstanding throughout 2024. On January 1, 2025, Copperfield repurchased 100,000 shares as treasury stock. No other share activity occurred. What is the effect of the buyback on Copperfield''s basic EPS trend from 2024 to 2025?', '["EPS remains unchanged at $4.00 because net income is constant", "EPS decreases from $4.00 to $3.33 due to higher weighted-average shares", "EPS increases from $4.00 to $5.00 due to reduced share count", "EPS increases from $4.00 to $4.44 after a partial-year adjustment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS uses weighted-average shares. In 2024: $2,000,000 / 500,000 = $4.00. In 2025, the January 1 repurchase reduces shares to 400,000 for the entire year: $2,000,000 / 400,000 = $5.00. The buyback increases EPS because identical net income is spread over fewer shares. Choice A is incorrect because while net income is constant, the reduced share count directly increases the per-share metric.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Income from continuing operations vs discontinued operations EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $600,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $150,000, resulting in net income of $450,000. Whitfield has 100,000 weighted-average shares and no dilutive securities. Which EPS amounts must Whitfield present under ASC 260?', '["Net income EPS of $4.50 presented on the face; no other EPS figures required", "Continuing operations EPS of $6.00 on the face only; all others disclosed in notes", "Continuing operations, discontinued operations, and net income EPS all required on the face", "Continuing operations EPS and net income EPS on the face; discontinued operations EPS on face or in notes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, EPS must be presented on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and for net income. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented either on the face or in the notes. Continuing operations EPS = $600,000 / 100,000 = $6.00; net income EPS = $450,000 / 100,000 = $4.50. Choice A is incorrect because EPS for income from continuing operations must also appear on the face.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Rights issue below fair value (bonus element adjustment)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Alderton Corp. had 200,000 shares outstanding and reported basic EPS of $5.00 in 2024. On March 1, 2025, Alderton offered shareholders a rights issue of one new share for every four shares held at $20 per share when the market price was $40. What adjustment must Alderton make to its previously reported 2024 EPS to reflect the bonus element of the rights issue?', '["2024 EPS must be retroactively reduced by applying a bonus adjustment factor", "No adjustment is needed because the rights are issued in 2025", "2024 EPS must be increased to reflect the premium on the rights offering", "2024 EPS is adjusted only if the rights are exercised before financial statements are issued"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-20 through 55-27, a rights issue below market price contains a bonus element similar to a stock dividend. The theoretical ex-rights price is calculated, and a bonus factor (market price / theoretical ex-rights price) is applied retroactively, increasing the weighted-average shares and reducing prior-period EPS. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment for the bonus element, analogous to stock dividend treatment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Partial-year convertible bond if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. has net income of $840,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, and basic EPS of $4.20. On July 1, 2025, Ferndale issued $600,000 of 8% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond converting into 15 shares. The tax rate is 25%. Using the if-converted method for diluted EPS, what amounts are added to the numerator and denominator for the bonds?', '["Numerator: $36,000; Denominator: 9,000 shares", "Numerator: $18,000; Denominator: 4,500 shares", "Numerator: $24,000; Denominator: 9,000 shares", "Numerator: $18,000; Denominator: 9,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, when convertible bonds are outstanding for only part of the period, the if-converted method applies from the issuance date. Interest saved = $600,000 x 8% x 6/12 = $24,000. After-tax adjustment = $24,000 x 0.75 = $18,000 added to numerator. Shares = 600 bonds x 15 = 9,000 x 6/12 = 4,500 weighted shares added to denominator. Choice A incorrectly uses full-year amounts as if the bonds were outstanding the entire year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Two-class method with participating preferred and undistributed earnings
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonebridge Holdings has 400,000 common shares and 100,000 participating preferred shares ($100 par, 5% stated rate) that participate equally with common in dividends beyond the stated rate. Net income is $3,000,000 and declared dividends total $1,000,000 ($500,000 to preferred, $500,000 to common). Evaluate how undistributed earnings should be allocated under the two-class method and determine basic EPS for common shareholders.', '["$6.25", "$4.00", "$5.25", "$7.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, undistributed earnings ($3,000,000 - $1,000,000 = $2,000,000) must be allocated between common and participating preferred based on their participation rights. Common receives 400,000/500,000 x $2,000,000 = $1,600,000. Total common earnings = $500,000 dividends + $1,600,000 = $2,100,000. Basic EPS = $2,100,000 / 400,000 = $5.25. Choice A ($6.25) incorrectly uses only the $500,000 stated dividend as the preferred deduction without allocating undistributed earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Assess whether share-based compensation awards affect diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prestige Financial granted 20,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) to employees on January 1, 2025, vesting over 3 years. The average unamortized compensation cost during 2025 was $700,000. The average market price was $60 per share. Prestige has basic EPS of $4.00 on 400,000 shares. Assess how these unvested RSUs affect diluted EPS under the treasury stock method per ASC 260.', '["RSUs add 20,000 shares to the denominator with no other adjustment", "RSUs are excluded from diluted EPS because they have not yet vested", "RSUs have no dilutive effect because restricted stock is not a potentially dilutive security", "RSUs add 8,333 incremental shares using the treasury stock method with assumed proceeds"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, unvested share-based compensation awards are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds equal the unamortized compensation cost ($700,000). Shares repurchased = $700,000 / $60 = 11,667. Incremental shares = 20,000 - 11,667 = 8,333 added to the denominator. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires unvested awards to be included in diluted EPS during the vesting period as potentially dilutive securities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Written put option effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ironwood Corp. has 300,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $1,200,000. The company wrote put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $50 per share. The average market price during the period was $40 per share. Under the reverse treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to diluted EPS?', '["2,500 incremental shares are added to the denominator", "No incremental shares; the written puts are antidilutive", "10,000 incremental shares are added to the denominator", "12,500 incremental shares are added to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes repurchase of 10,000 shares at $50 = $500,000, then reissuance at market: $500,000 / $40 = 12,500 shares. Incremental shares = 12,500 - 10,000 = 2,500. Choice B is incorrect because when the put exercise price exceeds market price, the written put is in-the-money and dilutive.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Complex scenario: net loss makes all securities antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. reports a net loss from continuing operations of $400,000 and income from discontinued operations of $150,000, yielding a net loss of $250,000. The company has 200,000 shares, 5,000 dilutive stock options, and $100,000 of 6% convertible bonds convertible into 8,000 shares. The controller proposes including the options and bonds in diluted EPS for the discontinued operations line. Evaluate this position under ASC 260.', '["The controller is correct; dilutive securities should be included for profitable line items", "The controller is incorrect; a loss from continuing operations makes all securities antidilutive for every EPS figure", "The controller is partially correct; only options should be included for discontinued operations", "The controller is incorrect; only the convertible bonds are antidilutive because they add to the numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations is the control number for determining dilutive effect. When a loss from continuing operations exists, including any potentially dilutive securities would reduce the loss per share (antidilutive), so all securities are excluded from diluted EPS for every line item, including discontinued operations showing income. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 uses continuing operations as the single control number, not individual line items.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be disclosed per ASC 260
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under GAAP", "Only entities with more than $10 million in total assets", "Only entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "All entities except not-for-profit organizations and governments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock such as options, warrants, or convertible securities traded in a public market. Private companies and non-public entities are not required to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so. Choice A is incorrect because the EPS requirement applies specifically to public entities, not all GAAP reporters.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — What is a potentially dilutive security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following is an example of a potentially dilutive security?', '["Treasury stock held by the issuing company", "Nonconvertible preferred stock with a fixed dividend", "Common shares currently outstanding on the balance sheet", "Stock options granted to employees"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, potentially dilutive securities are instruments that could result in the issuance of additional common shares, thereby reducing EPS. Stock options, warrants, convertible bonds, and convertible preferred stock are common examples. Treasury stock and currently outstanding common shares do not create potential dilution. Choice B (nonconvertible preferred) affects the numerator through dividend deductions but does not create additional share issuances.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred antidilutive test calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brentwood Corp. has basic EPS of $2.50, based on net income of $625,000 and 250,000 weighted-average shares after deducting $75,000 in preferred dividends. The preferred stock (7,500 shares, $10 annual dividend) is convertible into 20,000 common shares. Applying the if-converted method, what is the per-share impact of the preferred, and is it dilutive?', '["$3.75 per share; antidilutive because it exceeds basic EPS of $2.50", "$3.75 per share; dilutive because it reduces the earnings available", "$2.78 per share; dilutive because it is below basic EPS of $2.50", "$2.78 per share; antidilutive because conversion is unfavorable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the convertible preferred per-share impact = dividends saved / additional shares = $75,000 / 20,000 = $3.75. Since $3.75 exceeds basic EPS of $2.50, including the preferred would increase EPS, making it antidilutive. The preferred is therefore excluded from diluted EPS. Choice B incorrectly identifies the $3.75 per-share impact as dilutive when it actually exceeds basic EPS and would raise the ratio.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Preferred dividends in arrears (multiple years)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halstead Manufacturing has net income of $1,200,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 10,000 shares of $100 par, 5% cumulative preferred stock. Preferred dividends have not been declared for the current year or the two prior years ($150,000 total in arrears). What preferred dividend amount is deducted from the numerator for basic EPS?', '["$150,000 representing all dividends in arrears", "$50,000 representing the current-year dividend only", "$100,000 representing prior years'' arrears only", "$0 because no dividends were declared"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, for cumulative preferred stock, only the current-period dividend ($50,000 = 10,000 x $100 x 5%) is deducted in computing basic EPS, regardless of whether declared. Prior-year arrears were already deducted in the years they related to and are not double-counted. Basic EPS = ($1,200,000 - $50,000) / 400,000 = $2.875. Choice A ($150,000) incorrectly includes prior-year arrears already deducted in earlier periods.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding represent?', '["Total authorized shares divided by the number of reporting periods", "The average of beginning and ending shares outstanding", "Shares outstanding weighted by the portion of the period they were outstanding", "The maximum number of shares outstanding at any point during the period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of common shares is computed by weighting each change in shares outstanding by the fraction of the reporting period those shares were outstanding. This time-weighted approach provides a more accurate measure of the capital base available throughout the period. Choice B (simple average of beginning and ending) would only be correct if all share changes occurred exactly at the midpoint.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Forward purchase contract effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Inc. has 250,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $1,000,000. The company entered a forward purchase contract to buy back 15,000 shares at $60 per share. The average market price during the period was $45 per share. Under ASC 260, how does this forward contract affect diluted EPS?', '["No effect; forward contracts are excluded from EPS computations", "15,000 shares are subtracted from the denominator directly", "The contract is antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS", "5,000 incremental shares are added to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, a forward purchase contract at a price above average market price is treated like a written put. The reverse treasury stock method assumes repurchase of 15,000 shares at $60 ($900,000), then reissuance at $45 = $900,000 / $45 = 20,000 shares. Incremental shares = 20,000 - 15,000 = 5,000 added to the denominator. Choice C is incorrect because the contract price exceeds market price, making it dilutive, not antidilutive.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — When dual presentation of basic and diluted EPS is required
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when must a public entity present both basic and diluted earnings per share?', '["Whenever the entity has a complex capital structure", "Only when diluted EPS differs from basic EPS by more than 5%", "Only in annual financial statements, not in interim reports", "Only when the entity reports a net loss for the period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a public entity must present both basic and diluted EPS whenever it has a complex capital structure with outstanding potentially dilutive securities. The dual presentation is required regardless of the magnitude of the difference between basic and diluted EPS. Choice B is incorrect because there is no materiality threshold; even minimal differences require dual presentation when potentially dilutive securities exist.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Cumulative preferred dividends always deducted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'For basic EPS, how are dividends on cumulative preferred stock treated when no dividends are declared during the period?', '["No deduction is made because dividends were not declared", "Only half of the annual dividend is deducted as an estimate", "The current-period cumulative dividend is still deducted from net income", "The cumulative dividend is added back to net income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, dividends on cumulative preferred stock are deducted from net income in computing basic EPS regardless of whether declared. Cumulative preferred holders have a claim to the dividend whether or not the board declares it, reducing earnings available to common shareholders. Choice A is incorrect because it describes the treatment for noncumulative preferred stock, where dividends are deducted only when actually declared by the board.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
